\" Generated by script2man from auto-disable-service
.TH auto-disable-service 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-disable-service - Disable a service

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-disable-service service-name
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
service-name    Name of the service as it appears in rc.conf (BSD)
or as it appears in a systemctl command (Linux),
or the name of a plist file with or without ".plist"
(macOS)
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-disable-service
command disables a service.  It is primarily a compatibility layer
to mask the extensive differences in how services are handled
between various Unix platforms such as BSD, Linux, and macOS.
This makes it possible to write portable programs that manipulate
services.

On BSD systems, the name of the
service as it appears in rc.conf should be used.  This may differ
from the script name in rc.d.

On macOS, use the plist name
with or without the .plist extension.

On Linux systems using systemd, use the service name as it would
appear in a systemctl command.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-disable-service sshd
.ad
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8), auto-enable-service(8), auto-restart-services(8),
auto-services-manager(8), auto-service-enabled(8)

